\section{Introduction}

The students present their labs (\enquote{redovisning}) online, to the TAs who
have booked the presentation sessions in nytid.
In Canvas the students book a time for this in an appointment group: the
session is cut into slots, 15 minutes for a lab and 30 for the P-uppgift, and
each slot takes a fixed number of students.
For the session on 5 October, 13:00--15:00, we want
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms calendar create -c prgi26 "Redovisning" \
    --start-time "2026-10-05 13:00" --end-time "2026-10-05 15:00" \
    --location "https://queue.eecs.kth.se/queue/Prgi" \
    --event-type appointment_group \
    --time-slot-duration 15 --max-appointments 4 \
    --description "marwahas samed mweng yasmines ealaton kfgruner"
\end{minted}
Six TAs have booked that session (besides me), so four students per slot.

This script derives such commands from the TA schedule in nytid.
It only \emph{prints} them: I review, edit and run them myself.
Creating an appointment group is not idempotent---running the same command
twice gives the students two groups to book in---so the script must never be
the one that sends them to Canvas.

We only create the slots one week in advance.
Before that, TAs still book and unbook sessions, so the number of students per
slot would be based on a staffing that has not yet stabilized.

\subsection{Running it weekly from cron}

That makes the script a good fit for a weekly cron job, for instance on
Monday mornings:
\begin{minted}{text}
0 8 * * 1 cd ~/devel/edu/intropy/adm/redovisningar && ./redovisningar.sh prgi26
\end{minted}
Cron mails me the output.
I open the mail in Vim, check each command, select it and run it with
[[:'<,'>w !bash]], which writes the selected lines to [[bash]] and leaves
the mail as it is.
The script still only prints; running a command remains my decision.

Each run covers the seven days from the day it runs, see
\cref{sec:schedule}, so consecutive weekly runs cover consecutive weeks and
never overlap.
No session is printed twice, so running the commands of every mail never
gives a session two appointment groups.
The defaults are chosen for this use: the title defaults to
\enquote{Redovisning} and the window to the coming week, so the cron line
needs no more than the course.

\subsection{The script}

The structure follows [[missing.sh]]: constants, functions and a [[main]]
that runs only when the script is executed, not when it is sourced for
debugging.
The script is run as
\begin{minted}{text}
redovisningar.sh [-d minutes] course [title] [nytid schedule show options]
\end{minted}
The course is used for both nytid and canvaslms.
The title is the name of the appointment group in Canvas, and it decides the
slot length, which [[-d]] overrides, see \cref{sec:title}.
The remaining arguments go to [[nytid schedule show]], see
\cref{sec:schedule}.
<<[[redovisningar.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<constants>>
<<functions>>

# Run the script if not sourced
if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "${0}" ]]; then
  main "$@"
fi
@

A typical run by hand is
\begin{minted}{bash}
./redovisningar.sh prgi26 "Redovisning P-uppgift" > redovisningar-v41.sh
\end{minted}
followed by editing the output and running it with [[bash]].
If the sessions need another slot length than their title gives, say
20 minutes, we run
\begin{minted}{bash}
./redovisningar.sh -d 20 prgi26 "Redovisning lab 3"
\end{minted}


\section{The presentation sessions}\label{sec:schedule}

[[nytid schedule show]] shows the events from today until a week from today
by default, and it includes the events of both dates.
That is eight days, not seven: a run on a Monday includes the next Monday,
which the run a week later includes again.
For the weekly cron job that would print the Monday sessions twice.
So we ask for an end date six days from today, which gives exactly one week.
We put our [[--end]] before the user's arguments: nytid, like any Click-based
program, takes the last value of an option given twice, so an [[--end]] among
the extra arguments overrides ours.
Extra arguments, such as [[--start 2026-10-12 --end 2026-10-18]], are passed
on to nytid that way.
When moving the window, give both dates: with only [[--start]], our end date
could fall before the start, and nytid then replaces it with the start plus a
week, which is eight days again.

We ask for [[--user ""]] to get all sessions and every TA booked on them, not
only my own bookings.
We ask for CSV with the end time and full dates, since canvaslms wants
[[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM]] for both ends of the session.
We also state [[--no-week]], [[--no-location]] and the delimiter explicitly,
although some of them are defaults: each of them can be changed in the nytid
configuration, and a changed default would shift the columns that we read
below.
<<constants>>=
TIME_FORMAT="%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"
WINDOW_DAYS=7
<<functions>>=
sessions() {
  nytid schedule show "$course" --user "" \
    --format csv --delimiter $'\t' \
    --no-week --no-location --no-todo \
    --end-time --time-format "$TIME_FORMAT" \
    --end "$(date -d "+$((WINDOW_DAYS - 1)) days" +%F)" \
    "$@" \
  | tr -d '\r' \
  | awk -F'\t' -v pattern="$SESSION_PATTERN" \
    'tolower($3) ~ pattern'
}
@

Python's [[csv]] module ends every row with CRLF, which [[tr]] removes;
otherwise the last field, and with it the last TA's username, would end in
a carriage return.

The rows then have the columns start, end, event and description.
The course has both lab sessions and presentation sessions; the presentation
sessions are the ones with \enquote{redovisning} in the event name, for
example \enquote{Datorlaboration redovisning online}.
We match on the event column only, since the description could mention a
presentation without being one.
<<constants>>=
SESSION_PATTERN="redovisning"
@


\section{The TAs of a session}

The description of a session looks like
\begin{minted}{text}
Needed TAs: 10; Booked TAs: dbosk marwahas samed mweng yasmines ealaton kfgruner
\end{minted}
and may continue with [[; Reserve TAs: ...]].
Reserves are not counted: they only work the session if a booked TA drops out.
We take the words between [[Booked TAs:]] and the next semicolon.

I am booked on the sessions too, but I do not take presentations, so I am
excluded both from the count and from the list of TAs in the description.
nytid identifies TAs by their KTH username, which on my machine is also
[[$USER]]; [[TEACHER]] can be set in the environment to override it.

A TA can appear twice in the list when they have booked the same session twice
in the sign-up sheet, as happened on 6 October 2026.
They still take only one student at a time, so we keep only the first
occurrence of each name ([[awk '!seen[$0]++']] keeps the order, unlike
[[sort -u]]).
<<constants>>=
TEACHER="${TEACHER:-$USER}"
<<functions>>=
booked_tas() {
  local description="$1"
  local ta
  [[ $description =~ Booked\ TAs:\ ([^;]*) ]] || return
  for ta in ${BASH_REMATCH[1]}; do
    if [ "$ta" != "$TEACHER" ]; then
      echo "$ta"
    fi
  done \
  | awk '!seen[$0]++'
}
@


\section{Students per slot}

Each slot should take fewer students than there are TAs: we save more than
20~per cent of the TAs' capacity.
That is, the number of students per slot is the largest integer strictly less
than \(0.8n\) for \(n\) TAs, which is \(\lfloor (4n - 1)/5 \rfloor\) in integer
arithmetic.
Six TAs give four students per slot, ten TAs give seven.
The plain \(\lfloor 4n/5 \rfloor\) would give four students for five TAs,
which saves exactly 20~per cent, not more.
<<functions>>=
students_per_slot() {
  local tas="$1"
  echo $(( (4 * tas - 1) / 5 ))
}
@

With no TA other than me, this is zero (or negative).
That happens, for instance, when only reserves have signed up besides me.
Canvas has no use for a slot that takes no students, so [[main]] prints a
comment instead of a command for such a session; being a comment, it does no
harm when the output is run.


\section{The title and the slot length}\label{sec:title}

We book several kinds of presentations in the same sessions: the labs and
the stages of the P-uppgift.
The title of the appointment group tells the students which one they book,
for example \enquote{Redovisning lab 3} or \enquote{Redovisning P-uppgift}.
Without a title we use plain \enquote{Redovisning}, as in the weekly cron
job.
<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_TITLE="Redovisning"
@

The kind of presentation also decides how long a slot should be.
A lab and the shorter stages of the P-uppgift, the specification
(\enquote{spec}) and the review (\enquote{granskning}), take 15 minutes.
Presenting the P-uppgift itself, a whole program, takes 30.
We derive the slot length from the title, so that the title is all we give
in the usual cases:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
  Title & Minutes \\
  \hline
  Redovisning & 15 \\
  Redovisning lab 3 & 15 \\
  Redovisning spec & 15 \\
  Redovisning granskning & 15 \\
  Redovisning P-uppgift & 30 \\
  Redovisning p-uppgift del 2 & 30 \\
  anything else & 15 \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The 15-minute rule gives the same as the default, but stating it records
which titles we know about.
A title outside both rules gets the default rather than an error, since the
commands are reviewed before they are run.

The regular expressions need care with anchoring.
The one for the 15-minute titles is anchored at the end: otherwise its
optional group would let it match every title that starts with
\enquote{Redovisning}, the P-uppgift ones included.
The one for the P-uppgift is anchored only at the start, so that a title
with more after it, such as a part number, still gets 30 minutes.
<<constants>>=
LAB_TITLES='^Redovisning ?(lab.*|spec|granskning)?$'
LAB_SLOT_DURATION=15
PROJECT_TITLES='^Redovisning [Pp]-uppgift'
PROJECT_SLOT_DURATION=30
DEFAULT_SLOT_DURATION=15
@

The expressions are kept in variables and used unquoted after [[=~]]: a
quoted right-hand side would be matched as a literal string, and writing the
expression inline would force us to escape its spaces.
<<functions>>=
slot_duration_for() {
  local title="$1"
  if [[ $title =~ $PROJECT_TITLES ]]; then
    echo "$PROJECT_SLOT_DURATION"
  elif [[ $title =~ $LAB_TITLES ]]; then
    echo "$LAB_SLOT_DURATION"
  else
    echo "$DEFAULT_SLOT_DURATION"
  fi
}
@


\section{The canvaslms command}

The location is the same for every session.
It is the queue system, where the students queue to the TA once their slot
starts.
<<constants>>=
LOCATION="https://queue.eecs.kth.se/queue/Prgi"
@

We print the command in the same layout as in the introduction, one option
group per line, so that it is easy to edit before running it.
The TAs go in the description; that way the students see who is working the
session.
The course, the title and the slot length are the same for all sessions of
a run; [[main]] sets them once, see \cref{sec:arguments}.
<<functions>>=
print_command() {
  local start="$1" end="$2" max="$3" tas="$4"
  printf 'canvaslms calendar create -c %s "%s" \\\n' "$course" "$title"
  printf '    --start-time "%s" --end-time "%s" \\\n' "$start" "$end"
  printf '    --location "%s" \\\n' "$LOCATION"
  printf '    --event-type appointment_group \\\n'
  printf '    --time-slot-duration %s --max-appointments %s \\\n' \
    "$slot_duration" "$max"
  printf '    --description "%s"\n' "$tas"
}
@


\section{The main function}

[[main]] reads the arguments, fetches the sessions and prints one command
per session, with a blank line between them.
<<functions>>=
main() {
  <<read the arguments>>
  local start end event description tas n max
  sessions "$@" \
  | while IFS=$'\t' read -r start end event description; do
      tas=$(booked_tas "$description" | tr '\n' ' ')
      tas="${tas% }"
      n=$(wc -w <<< "$tas")
      max=$(students_per_slot "$n")
      if [ "$max" -lt 1 ]; then
        echo "# $start to $end $event: no TA but $TEACHER, skipping"
      else
        print_command "$start" "$end" "$max" "$tas"
      fi
      echo
    done
}
@

\subsection{Reading the arguments}\label{sec:arguments}

[[-d]] must come before the course.
Everything after the title goes to nytid unchanged, so an option there is
nytid's; [[getopts]] stops at the first argument that is not an option
anyway.
[[OPTIND]] is local so that [[main]] can be called more than once when the
script is sourced for debugging.
We accept only a positive whole number of minutes, since a slot of zero
minutes, or of a fraction of one, means nothing to canvaslms.
<<read the arguments>>=
local opt OPTIND override=""
while getopts d: opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    d) override="$OPTARG" ;;
    *) usage; return 1 ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND - 1))
if [[ -n $override && ! $override =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]]; then
  usage
  return 1
fi
@

The course is required.
The title is optional, yet it comes before the options to nytid.
We tell the two apart by the leading dash: everything we give nytid is an
option, such as [[--start]], and no title starts with a dash.
That keeps the cron line short, and a run by hand can still move the window
without naming the title.
<<read the arguments>>=
if [ $# -lt 1 ]; then
  usage
  return 1
fi
course="$1"
shift
if [[ -n $1 && $1 != -* ]]; then
  title="$1"
  shift
else
  title="$DEFAULT_TITLE"
fi
slot_duration="${override:-$(slot_duration_for "$title")}"
@

The usage message goes to standard error, so that it never ends up among the
commands when the output is redirected to a file or mailed by cron.
[[main]] returns after it rather than exits, which keeps a shell that sourced
the script alive.
<<functions>>=
usage() {
  echo "usage: ${0##*/} [-d minutes] course [title]" \
    "[nytid schedule show options]" >&2
}
@

